1
00:00:01,035 --> 00:00:02,300
This is Control Center.

2
00:00:02,347 --> 00:00:05,621
It takes a business requirement all the way to release.

3
00:00:07,335 --> 00:00:09,680
Here is what really happens on a project.

4
00:00:09,796 --> 00:00:11,956
One story, three developers.

5
00:00:12,002 --> 00:00:15,961
The first pastes the ticket into their AI assistant.

6
00:00:15,984 --> 00:00:18,910
The second reads a README from two years ago.

7
00:00:18,956 --> 00:00:20,849
The third has only the title.

8
00:00:20,930 --> 00:00:23,264
Same story, three different contexts.

9
00:00:23,357 --> 00:00:25,063
So three different answers.

10
00:00:27,635 --> 00:00:32,534
Now the same requirement goes in once, and out comes a set of files.

11
00:00:32,569 --> 00:00:33,289
The epic.

12
00:00:33,335 --> 00:00:34,554
Four user stories.

13
00:00:34,612 --> 00:00:41,265
Twelve acceptance criteria, each a numbered, testable sentence about what done actually means.

14
00:00:41,335 --> 00:00:42,298
The architecture.

15
00:00:42,356 --> 00:00:45,247
And one failing test for every criterion.

16
00:00:45,282 --> 00:00:49,822
All three developers now read the same pack, and all three get it right.

17
00:00:51,336 --> 00:00:53,833
The work splits into two halves.

18
00:00:53,891 --> 00:00:56,619
The first half is upstream: agree what done means.

19
00:00:56,642 --> 00:01:02,052
The requirement is read, the design is drawn, the plan is written, and a named human signs it.

20
00:01:02,087 --> 00:01:05,187
Every step leaves a file behind, not a conversation.

21
00:01:10,636 --> 00:01:15,164
The second half is downstream: build until it is provably done.

22
00:01:15,199 --> 00:01:17,637
The pack lands on a fresh branch.

23
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:21,062
The developer's agent takes one criterion at a time.

24
00:01:21,387 --> 00:01:22,931
Real tests run.

25
00:01:23,106 --> 00:01:28,481
A second model reviews the work before any human is asked to look at it.

26
00:01:30,095 --> 00:01:31,616
Here is the developer's side.

27
00:01:31,662 --> 00:01:36,271
They say, start working, and the agent creates the branch and stops.

28
00:01:36,329 --> 00:01:41,682
They say, plan the next criterion, and it writes a plan for exactly one.

29
00:01:41,716 --> 00:01:43,946
Then the developer edits that plan.

30
00:01:43,969 --> 00:01:50,807
That edit is the contract, and the agent builds what the file now says, not what it proposed.

31
00:01:52,305 --> 00:01:55,080
This is the real application, not a mock-up.

32
00:01:55,114 --> 00:01:57,332
This is Gate One, plan sign-off.

33
00:01:57,413 --> 00:02:02,870
Nothing downstream generates, publishes, or runs until a named person clicks.

34
00:02:02,986 --> 00:02:04,495
A click is a decision.

35
00:02:04,553 --> 00:02:06,957
Typing yes at a prompt is not.

36
00:02:12,605 --> 00:02:14,497
Every artifact says what made it.

37
00:02:14,590 --> 00:02:17,760
Live AI, rule based, simulated, or human.

38
00:02:17,794 --> 00:02:26,641
A deterministic script is never dressed up as AI, and simulated work is never counted as AI work anywhere.

39
00:02:28,265 --> 00:02:29,263
And it runs.

40
00:02:29,321 --> 00:02:33,304
One requirement went from intake to a released application overnight.

41
00:02:33,385 --> 00:02:39,225
Real repository, real pull request, merged, 98.5% test coverage.

42
00:02:41,565 --> 00:02:43,388
Governance is the product.

43
00:02:43,480 --> 00:02:48,368
Not how much the AI writes, but how many places a human has to sign.

